{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"plaid-glass-manxcat","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Symptom\n\nThe `Live External Integration` workflow's `jira DC harness smoke` job fails on\n[run 35508005436](https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/35508005436) because the\nGitHub-hosted runner runs out of disk, not because anything about Jira or the bridge is\nwrong.\n\nThe runner warns first, then git fails, then the runner's own logging fails:\n\n```\n##[warning]You are running out of disk space. The runner will stop working when the\nmachine runs out of disk space. Free space left: 23 MB\n\nfatal: sha1 file '/home/runner/work/_temp/rebar-basetemp/test_promote_only_binding_is_o0/\ndc-multi-store-copy/.tickets-tracker/.git/index.lock' write error. Out of diskspace\n\nsubprocess.CalledProcessError: Command '['git', 'commit', '-q', '--no-verify', '-m',\n'scrubbed store copy for 368f DC rehearsal']' returned non-zero exit status 128\n\n##[error]No space left on device :\n'/home/runner/actions-runner/cached/2.337.0/_diag/pages/491cc8e3-...log'\n```\n\nSo the `git commit` exit 128 is ENOSPC with its own stderr naming it, rather than a\ngit-semantics failure — the diagnosis does not rest on inference.\n\nThe job otherwise reached a healthy state before dying: Jira DC came up and four projects\n(`RBJFTJP`, `RBJYIPQ`, `RBJUAGO`, `RBJQDHV`) provisioned their Epic fields in ~0.03s each.\nThe harness is fine. It is the disk that is not.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe DC rehearsal fixtures copy the **entire live tickets branch** into the runner's temp\ndirectory, once per test, and each copy costs roughly twice the store's working-tree size.\n\n`tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_multi_project_rehearsal.py:210-237`:\n\n```python\nwork = tmp_path / \"dc-multi-store-copy\"\n...\narchive = run_git([\"git\", \"archive\", \"FETCH_HEAD\"], cwd=source).stdout\nsubprocess.run([\"tar\", \"-x\", \"-C\", str(tracker)], input=archive, check=True)\nscrub_bridge_state(tracker)\n_init(tracker, \"tickets\")\nsubprocess.run([\"git\", \"add\", \"-A\"], cwd=tracker, check=True)\nsubprocess.run([\"git\", \"commit\", ...], cwd=tracker, check=True)\n```\n\nThree costs stack, all proportional to store size and none bounded:\n\n1. `git archive FETCH_HEAD` materializes the **whole branch as a byte string in RAM**\n   (`.stdout`) before anything is written.\n2. `tar -x` writes a full working-tree copy to disk.\n3. `git add -A` + `git commit` then writes a **second** full copy into the new repo's\n   object database, so each fixture instance costs ~2x the tree on disk.\n\nThe store measured **1.5 GB** of working tree on 2026-09-20. A hosted `ubuntu-24.04`\nrunner has roughly 14 GB free after the image, and this job additionally hosts a\nDockerized Jira Data Center plus its database. Three separate modules perform this copy —\n`test_multi_project_rehearsal.py`, `test_dc_mutations.py`, and\n`test_store_copy_isolation.py` — each into its own `tmp_path`, and pytest retains recent\n`tmp_path` trees rather than deleting them immediately.\n\nThis is why the failure is new rather than a flake: the cost is a function of store size,\nand the store grows monotonically with every ticket event. The job has been getting closer\nto the limit on every run and has now crossed it. It will not recover on its own, and a\nrerun is not a remedy.\n\n## Approach\n\nStop copying the whole store to exercise a store copy. The fixtures need a scrubbed,\nwritable store that is representative — not a byte-complete replica of every ticket ever\nwritten. Two independent reductions, either of which alone restores headroom, and which\ncompose:\n\n- Bound what is copied, so cost stops tracking total store size.\n- Avoid paying for the copy twice by not re-committing a full working tree into a fresh\n  object database when a cheap history-free copy would do.\n\nReclaiming each fixture's tree once the test finishes keeps concurrent modules from\nsumming their peaks.\n\nThe fix must not weaken what these tests prove: they exist to round-trip a **real**\nscrubbed store through live Jira DC, so substituting synthetic tickets would relocate the\ncoverage gap rather than close it.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Peak disk used by a DC rehearsal fixture does not scale with total store size. Verified by running the fixture against two stores of very different sizes and comparing peak usage.\n- [ ] The store snapshot is not buffered whole in memory. Verified by reading the changed code for the `git archive` stdout capture.\n- [ ] Each fixture's copy is reclaimed when its test finishes, so concurrent modules do not sum their peaks. Verified by asserting the path is gone after teardown.\n- [ ] The tests still round-trip a REAL scrubbed store against live Jira DC, not synthetic tickets. Verified by reading the fixture for the live-branch source.\n- [ ] The job completes without a runner disk warning. Verified by the absence of `running out of disk space` in a green run's log.\n- [ ] The fix requires no single CI provider. Verified by reading the changed code for GitHub Actions specifics; the fixtures must behave the same locally.\n\n## Verification\n\nA green `Live External Integration` run whose log contains no\n`You are running out of disk space` warning, plus a recorded `df` delta for the rehearsal\nstep showing peak usage independent of store size.\n","id":"25e0-4d88-83d9-4253","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Jira DC harness exhausts the runner disk by copying the whole 1.5 GB ticket store per test"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789919545534604073,"uuid":"035d37d7-87e5-456f-8108-e6a9b5c75471"}